CAT = "rdResearch"; SEG = "RES"
Q = [
("Which study design best shows that a nutrition intervention causes an outcome?",
 "Randomized controlled trial", ["Prospective cohort study", "Case-control study", "Cross-sectional survey"],
 "Random assignment balances known and unknown confounders between groups, so a difference in outcome can be attributed to the intervention. Observational designs show association only."),
("Researchers compare past diets of people with colon cancer and matched people without it. What design is this?",
 "Case-control study", ["Cohort study", "Clinical trial", "Ecological study"],
 "A case-control study starts from the outcome and looks back at exposures. It reports an odds ratio; a cohort study follows exposed and unexposed people forward and reports relative risk."),
("A study concludes a supplement lowers LDL when, in fact, it has no effect. What error is this?",
 "Type I error", ["Type II error", "Sampling bias", "Recall bias"],
 "A Type I error rejects a true null hypothesis (a false positive); its chance is the alpha level, often 0.05. A Type II error misses a real effect, often because the sample is too small."),
("A screening tool flags 72 of the 80 malnourished patients in a sample. What is its sensitivity?",
 "90%", ["72%", "80%", "10%"],
 "Sensitivity is true positives / all who have the condition: 72 / 80 = 0.90. The 8 missed are false negatives, which is 10% of the malnourished patients."),
("Before a study enrolls hospital patients, which body must review it to protect participants?",
 "Institutional Review Board", ["Food and Drug Administration", "Joint Commission surveyors", "Hospital billing office"],
 "An IRB reviews research with human subjects for risks, informed consent and privacy before it begins. The FDA becomes involved only for certain drug, device or food additive studies."),
("In a study of sodium intake and blood pressure, which is the dependent variable?",
 "Blood pressure", ["Sodium intake", "Age of subjects", "Study location"],
 "The dependent variable is the outcome measured; the independent variable (sodium intake) is the one changed or compared. Age is a possible confounder to control for."),
("A set of daily intakes is strongly skewed by a few very high values. Which measure best describes the typical intake?",
 "Median", ["Mean", "Range", "Standard deviation"],
 "The median is the middle value and is not pulled by outliers, so it suits skewed data. The mean is pulled toward the extreme values, and range and SD describe spread, not the center."),
("A scale gives the same weight each time a patient steps on it, but it reads 2 kg too high. How would you describe it?",
 "Reliable but not valid", ["Valid but not reliable", "Both valid and reliable", "Neither valid nor reliable"],
 "Reliability is consistency; validity is accuracy. A scale that repeats the same wrong value is reliable but biased, so its readings are not valid until it is recalibrated."),
("Which statistical test compares mean weight loss across three diet groups?",
 "Analysis of variance", ["Chi-square test", "Paired t-test", "Pearson correlation"],
 "ANOVA compares means of three or more groups. A t-test compares two means, chi-square compares counts in categories, and correlation measures how two continuous variables move together."),
("A study finds r = 0.85 between fruit intake and serum vitamin C. What can be concluded?",
 "A strong positive association", ["Fruit intake causes the level", "A weak negative association", "No relationship at all"],
 "An r near +1 shows a strong positive linear association, but correlation alone does not prove cause. A negative r would mean one rises as the other falls."),
("In a meta-analysis forest plot, the pooled diamond's confidence interval crosses the line of no effect (relative risk 1.0). What does this show?",
 "No significant pooled effect", ["A significant benefit", "A significant harm", "Proof the trials were biased"],
 "When the 95% confidence interval of the pooled estimate includes 1.0, the combined result is not statistically significant. A diamond wholly to one side of the line shows a significant effect in that direction."),
("In a trial, patients who stopped the diet are still analyzed in the group they were assigned to. What is this called?",
 "Intention-to-treat analysis", ["Per-protocol analysis", "Crossover design analysis", "Subgroup analysis"],
 "Intention-to-treat keeps everyone in the group they were randomized to, which preserves the balance randomization created. Per-protocol analysis includes only those who followed the plan."),
]
